<DocsPage id="permissions">

          <p>
            Every tool action is checked against a ruleset whose effect is <strong>allow</strong>, <strong>ask</strong> or <strong>deny</strong>. Rules from the agent, from the project and from your saved answers are merged: deny wins, then ask, then allow, and anything with no matching rule defaults to ask. An agent with no permissions at all is denied everything rather than trusted.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you answer <strong>always</strong>, that allow is saved for the project, so the same action is not asked again. Saved answers are per project, and you can clear them.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            For unattended runs the terminal agent takes <code>--auto</code> (also spelled <code>--yolo</code> or <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code>). It auto-approves anything not explicitly denied — deny rules still hold. It is off by default, and it is the flag to think twice about.
          </p>

          <h2 id="project-trust">Project trust</h2>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, a project's own <code>vector.json</code> and <code>.vector</code> folder (its agents,
            commands, modes, plugins, tools, skills, MCP servers, formatters and language servers) load only after you
            trust the project, because they can run code on your computer. Until then Vector applies the project's
            deny rules, and its run limits, loop pauses and sharing settings only where they are stricter than yours, so
            a cloned repository cannot raise your spend cap or switch loop pauses off. Nothing carries over from
            earlier versions, so after updating every project asks once. In the app, choose
            <strong>Trust project</strong> beside <strong>Project settings ignored</strong> in the session header.
            The TUI's <code>/trust</code> and the terminal's <code>vector trust status</code> and
            <code>vector trust approve</code> arrive with the next CLI release, 1.99.108 or later; the CLI that npm and
            <code>vectordev.ai/install</code> serve today has neither, and Windows WSL servers keep the 1.99.99 engine,
            without project trust, until a later desktop release requires that CLI.
          </p>
          <p>
            The language servers and formatters you turn on in your own settings also stay off in a project you have not
            trusted, because they run its code, and your own local MCP servers start in an empty folder outside it, so
            the project's <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.npmrc</code> or Python modules cannot take them over. This
            holds in every new project, one with no settings of its own included; there the app shows
            <strong>Project not trusted</strong> instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            Trust covers the project as it was when you approved it. A later change to anything that can run, from a
            pull or an edit, needs approval again; plans, memory and rules that Vector writes into <code>.vector</code>
            do not. Integrations you add from the app are kept with your own settings rather than in the project, so
            they stay after a restart. A trusted project can add allow rules, but it cannot lift a deny in your own
            settings.
          </p>

          <h2 id="read-only-specialists">Read-only specialists</h2>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, the read-only specialists (explore, review and security) ask before they run a test
            or build command such as <code>bun test</code>, <code>npm test</code>, <code>pytest</code>,
            <code>cargo test</code> or <code>go test</code>, because those run the repository's own code. Approve once,
            or approve always for the rest of the session. In an unattended <code>vector run</code> they are refused
            unless you pass <code>--auto</code>, and git and pull request reads still need no approval. The terminal
            agent gets this with the next CLI release, and Windows WSL servers once a later desktop release requires
            that CLI.
          </p>

          <h2 id="private-network">Your own network</h2>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, when an agent's web fetch would reach your own computer or network (a dev server on
            <code>localhost</code>, an intranet page, a cloud metadata address), Vector asks for
            <code>private_network</code> as well as <code>webfetch</code>, so a hostile page cannot steer the agent
            into your network. Approve it once for that address, or allow it in your settings, for example
            <code>"permission": {"{"} "private_network": {"{"} "http://localhost:3000": "allow" {"}"} {"}"}</code>.
            Addresses you put in your own settings (remote instructions, skill catalogs, providers such as a LAN Ollama
            host, MCP servers, references) are reached without asking. The terminal agent gets this with the next CLI
            release, and Windows WSL servers once a later desktop release requires that CLI.
          </p>

          <h2 id="shell-sandbox">The shell sandbox</h2>
          <p>
            Commands an agent runs can be confined to their workspace by the operating system's own mechanism — a
            seatbelt profile on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux where it is installed — by setting
            <code>VECTOR_SHELL_SANDBOX</code> from 1.99.99. Releases before 1.99.99, such as desktop
            1.99.8 and npm CLI 1.99.7, do not read that name, so setting it there confines nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            It is opt-in and off by default, and Windows ships no equivalent, so on Windows Vector reports that it is
            running unconfined instead of implying protection it cannot provide.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
